.TH ZSTD-SPLITTER 1 "2026-07-25" "zstd-splitter 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zstd-splitter \- create, compress, split, join, and verify tar archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zstd-splitter
.B -c
.BI -s " size"
.RB [ -e " engine" ]
.RB [ -l " level" ]
.RB [ -T " threads" ]
.RB [ -f ]
.I source
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -j
.RB [ -f ]
.I part
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -E
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.B -h
.PP
.B zstd-splitter
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zstd-splitter
creates a tar stream from one file or directory, compresses that stream with a
selected compression engine, and divides the compressed stream into numbered
parts.
It can later concatenate those parts and verify the reconstructed compressed
archive before publishing it.
.PP
Zstandard is the default compression engine for backward compatibility, but
.BR gzip (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR xz (1),
.BR lzip (1),
.BR lzop (1),
and
.BR lz4 (1)
formats are also supported when their corresponding programs are installed.
.PP
The command-line interface uses POSIX-style short options parsed with
.BR getopts .
Options must precede operands.
The special operand
.B --
ends option processing.
The long forms
.B --help
and
.B --engines
are convenience aliases and are not POSIX short options.
.PP
When invoked without arguments on an interactive terminal, the program displays
a text menu.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B -c
Create a tar archive, compress it, and split it into parts.
Exactly one
.I source
operand and the
.B -s
option are required.
.TP
.B -j
Join all parts belonging to the selected archive and verify the reconstructed
compressed stream.
Exactly one
.I part
operand is required.
The compression engine is inferred from the filename extension.
.TP
.B -E
List supported compression engines, filename extensions, compression-level
ranges, and thread support.
.TP
.B -h
Write the built-in help text to standard output and exit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -e " engine"
Select the compression engine used by
.BR -c .
The default is
.BR zstd .
Accepted engine names are:
.BR zstd ,
.BR gzip ,
.BR bzip2 ,
.BR xz ,
.BR lzma ,
.BR lzip ,
.BR lzop ,
and
.BR lz4 .
The aliases
.BR zst ,
.BR gz ,
.BR bz2 ,
and
.B lzo
are also accepted.
.TP
.BI -s " size"
Set the maximum size of each output part.
This option is mandatory with
.BR -c .
.I size
may be a positive byte count or an integer followed by
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T ,
or
.BR P .
The suffix may optionally be followed by
.B B
or
.BR iB .
All units are interpreted as powers of 1024.
Examples include
.BR 104857600 ,
.BR 100M ,
and
.BR 2GiB .
.TP
.BI -l " level"
Set the compression level.
If omitted, an engine-specific default is used.
Valid ranges are documented in the
.B ENGINES
section.
.TP
.BI -T " threads"
Set the number of compression worker threads for
.BR zstd ,
.BR xz ,
or
.BR lzma .
A value of 0 asks the engine to select automatically.
This option is rejected for engines without thread support.
.TP
.B -f
Replace existing output without an interactive confirmation prompt.
When standard input is not a terminal, replacement is refused unless
.B -f
is supplied.
.TP
.B --
End option processing.
Use this before a source or part pathname beginning with a hyphen.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I source
The file or directory to place in the tar archive.
The source is archived using a relative entry name based on its basename.
Archiving the filesystem root directory directly is refused.
.TP
.I part
Any member of a part set whose name has the form:
.PP
.RS
.I name.tar.extension.part.aaaaaa
.RE
.PP
The selected part need not be the first part.
All matching six-letter suffixes in the same directory are concatenated in
shell collation order under the C locale.
.SH OUTPUT FILES
For compression, output parts are created beside the source and use one of the
following forms:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name.tar.zst.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.gz.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.bz2.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.xz.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.lzma.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.lz.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.lzo.part.aaaaaa
name.tar.lz4.part.aaaaaa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Subsequent parts use suffixes such as
.B aaaaab
and
.BR aaaaac .
.PP
Joining removes the final
.B .part.aaaaaa
component and creates the reconstructed archive beside the parts.
The existing parts are not deleted.
.SH ENGINES
.TS
l l l l l.
Engine	Extension	Command	Levels	Threads
zstd	.tar.zst	zstd	1-22	yes
gzip	.tar.gz	gzip	1-9	no
bzip2	.tar.bz2	bzip2	1-9	no
xz	.tar.xz	xz	0-9	yes
lzma	.tar.lzma	xz	0-9	yes
lzip	.tar.lz	lzip	0-9	no
lzop	.tar.lzo	lzop	1-9	no
lz4	.tar.lz4	lz4	1-12	no
.TE
.PP
Default compression levels are 3 for zstd, 6 for gzip, 9 for bzip2, 6 for xz
and lzma, 6 for lzip, 3 for lzop, and 1 for lz4.
.PP
An engine is available only when its external command can be found through
.BR PATH .
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
When no arguments are supplied and standard input is a terminal, the program
offers the following operations:
.PP
.RS
.nf
1) Compress and split
2) Join parts
3) List compression engines
q) Quit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Interactive compression asks for the source, engine, and maximum part size.
It uses the selected engine's default compression level and automatic thread
selection where applicable.
.SH INTEGRITY AND PUBLICATION
Compression is performed through named pipes in a private temporary directory.
The program waits for
.BR tar ,
the compressor, and
.B split
to finish independently.
It then concatenates the temporary parts and runs the selected engine's archive
test command before moving the parts into their final locations.
.PP
During joining, parts are first concatenated into a temporary archive.
The reconstructed stream is tested with the detected compression engine before
the final archive replaces any existing output.
.PP
These checks verify the compressed stream, not the semantic contents of every
tar member.
For stronger archival assurance, maintain an independent cryptographic manifest
for the source files or the generated parts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully, help was displayed, an engine
list was displayed, or the user declined an interactive replacement.
.TP
.B 1
An operational error occurred, such as a missing dependency, failed compression,
failed archive verification, inaccessible file, or temporary-file error.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage was invalid, an option or operand was rejected, or a menu
selection was unknown.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Used to locate tar, split, the selected compressor, and the other required
external utilities.
.TP
.B LC_ALL
The script sets
.B LC_ALL=C
internally to make suffix matching and part ordering deterministic.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Not used.
Temporary working directories are created beside the output so final publication
can use same-filesystem renames.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
The script requires a POSIX-compatible
.B /bin/sh
and the following base utilities:
.BR tar ,
.BR split ,
.BR cat ,
.BR mkdir ,
.BR rm ,
.BR mv ,
.BR dirname ,
.BR basename ,
.BR mkfifo ,
and
.BR awk .
It also requires the command associated with the chosen compression engine.
.PP
Although the shell syntax and short-option interface are POSIX-oriented, the
precise option sets of tar and the compression programs depend on their local
implementations.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create Zstandard-compressed parts of at most 500 MiB:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -c -s 500M "/path/My directory"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create gzip-compressed 100 MiB parts at level 9:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -c -e gzip -s 100M -l 9 disk-image.raw
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create xz-compressed 2 GiB parts using automatic thread selection:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -c -e xz -s 2GiB -l 6 -T 0 source-directory
.fi
.RE
.PP
Join a gzip archive by naming any one of its parts:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -j "/path/My directory.tar.gz.part.aaaaac"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Allow replacement without prompting:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -j -f archive.tar.xz.part.aaaaaa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Archive a pathname beginning with a hyphen:
.PP
.RS
.nf
zstd-splitter -c -s 100M -- "-source-file"
.fi
.RE
.SH INSTALLATION
A system-wide installation may use:
.PP
.RS
.nf
install -m 0755 zstd-splitter.sh /usr/local/bin/zstd-splitter
install -m 0644 zstd-splitter.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/zstd-splitter.1
mandb
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B mandb
step is optional on systems that update the manual-page index automatically.
A compressed manual page may be installed as
.B zstd-splitter.1.gz
instead.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/zstd-splitter
Suggested system-wide executable location.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/zstd-splitter.1
Suggested local manual-page location.
.SH NOTES
The program name is retained for compatibility even though the current version
supports compression engines other than Zstandard.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1),
.BR split (1),
.BR zstd (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR xz (1),
.BR lzip (1),
.BR lzop (1),
.BR lz4 (1),
.BR sha256sum (1)
